-- NEGATIVE Int literal in a VALUE-LIFT position. Expected exit code 17
--
-- This is D120's other half (plan 0.73 F3, D124). D120 folded `-5` into one
-- literal in the INFER realm only, so `- <literal>` had no rule in `⊢ᵍ` — the
-- closed-value family a literal is checked against when it sits at a
-- pure-arrow target. The result was that
--
--     emitLit = compose emit@E (-5)
--
-- failed with `Type mismatch: expected (Unit ω→ Int) but got Int`, while the
-- identical program with `5` compiled. `g-neg-int` closes it, and the float
-- twin `g-neg-float` lands with it so the two types cannot drift apart again.
--
-- THE COMPOSE SHAPE IS THE POINT, and it is why this is not another
-- `let _ = … in`: a `let`-bound effect compiles to a thunk that is never
-- entered, so it would exercise the elaborator but not the lowering. Here the
-- SigOp is genuinely invoked with a negative constant.
--
-- The parentheses are load-bearing: `-` is both prefix and infix, and an
-- application ends at the first token that cannot start an atom, so
-- `compose emit@E -5` would parse as the SUBTRACTION `(compose emit@E) - 5`.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

emitLit : IO Unit
emitLit = compose emit@E (\_ -> -5)

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose (\_ -> 17) emitLit)
